# tabulated-calendar v1
# key: chinese
# basis: tabulated
# fallback:
# month_count: 12
# source: Hong Kong Observatory, "Gregorian-Lunar Calendar Conversion Table",
#   official per-year text files T{year}e.txt for 1901..2100
#   (www.hko.gov.hk/en/gts/time/conversion.htm and .../calendar/text/files/).
#   HKO computes month starts from the astronomical new moon (Beijing/Chinese
#   standard-time midnight boundary); a leap month appears as a repeated month
#   number. Files archived under papers/calendars/hko_gregorian_lunar/.
# retrieved: 2026-07-21
# coverage: lunar years 1901 .. 2099 (Chinese New Year 2100 is the terminal sentinel; a complete lunar year has 12 or 13 months of 29 or 30 days)
# generation: for every month-start line in each T{year}e.txt (the lunar-date
#   field reads "Nth Lunar Month/month" instead of a day number), record its
#   Gregorian date as the month start; a leap month is the second consecutive
#   line with the same month number; the lunar-year number is the Gregorian year
#   of the non-leap 1st Lunar Month that opens it. Verified: every complete
#   lunar year has 12 or 13 months, every month 29 or 30 days, and to_jdn/
#   from_jdn round-trip across the whole range.
# gold: Chinese New Year 2024 (year jiachen) == 2024-02-10 (2024 month 1 day 1);
#   CNY 2025 == 2025-01-29; leap month 2 in 2023; leap month 6 in 2025
# encoding: ordinary months 1..12; a leap month following month M is addressed
#   as month M+100 (leap-6 of 2025 -> month 106), keeping ordinary month numbers
#   stable across leap and common years
# columns: year month leap jdn_start
